/* ═══════════ Para la clase por voz (clase.html) ═══════════
   EN_HCM: las reglas y las instrucciones en inglés sencillo, para los
   carriles medio y alto (desde A2 EFA ya explica en inglés).
   MISIONES_HCM: una misión hablada por lección, con su personaje.     */
var EN_HCM={
  1:{regla:"Use be + verb-ing for plans that are already confirmed.",porque:"Present progressive with a future time means an organized plan."},
  2:{regla:"After because, use a subject and a verb. Taste, look, smell, sound and feel go with an adjective, not an adverb.",porque:"Tastes needs an adjective, like good. Because needs a subject and a verb."},
  3:{regla:"Use was or were plus verb-ing for the long action. Use when plus the simple past for the interruption.",porque:"The long action goes in past progressive. The interruption goes in simple past."},
  4:{regla:"To report an order, say: tell someone to do something. To report a request, say: ask someone to do something. To compare, use the same as, similar to, different from, like and alike.",porque:"After tell or ask plus a person, the verb goes with to."},
  5:{regla:"No new grammar today. We review the words from lessons 1 to 4 in three scenes: the repair shop, life with a roommate, and a white lie.",porque:"After can, use the base verb. A confirmed plan goes in present progressive."},
  6:{regla:"Should gives advice. Could gives a possibility. Both go with the base verb. Adverbs of manner end in -ly and say how you do something: He drives carefully.",porque:"After should or could, no to. To say how, use an -ly adverb."},
  7:{regla:"Many verbs go with that plus a sentence. If the first verb is in the past, the second part is in the past too.",porque:"After a verb in the past, the that clause also uses the past."},
  8:{regla:"To apologize, say: I apologize for plus verb-ing. To show feelings, say: I'm sorry that plus a sentence.",porque:"After for, always use verb-ing. After sorry, use that plus a sentence."},
  10:{regla:"Use how to plus a verb to ask for or give instructions. Never drop the to.",porque:"After how, always use to plus the base verb."},
  11:{regla:"After before, after, until, when and while, use the present, even for the future: Sue exercises before she goes to work. Be supposed to means what people expect from you.",porque:"After before, after, when, until and while, the verb is in the present."},
  12:{regla:"To say where something is: above, across from, behind, below, next to, near, in front of, under. After enjoy, finish, like, mind and start, the verb goes in -ing.",porque:"Enjoy needs verb-ing, and in front of the bank is across from, not across."},
  13:{regla:"After afraid, glad, happy, pleased, ready, sorry and upset, use to plus a verb: I'm glad to meet you. We use it to introduce people.",porque:"These adjectives need to plus a verb. After let me, use the base verb."},
  14:{regla:"For fixed schedules, like buses, planes, classes and formations, use the simple present, not will: The bus arrives at six tomorrow.",porque:"A published schedule is a fixed fact, so it goes in simple present."},
  15:{regla:"Use would you or could you for polite requests. To report an order, use told or asked plus a person plus to.",porque:"After asked or told plus a person, use to plus the verb, never that."},
  16:{regla:"Be able to means can, but it works in every tense.",porque:"Could to does not exist. Use was or were able to for one specific time in the past."},
  17:{regla:"Use how far for distance and how long for time. Use it as the subject for weather, distance and time.",porque:"An English sentence needs a subject. It is the impersonal subject."}
};
